1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,189
This video runs the full pipeline on three more regular expressions. Each one has a larger alphabet.

2
00:00:07,639 --> 00:00:21,933
The first is the Under Pressure machine from the course notes. The other two are the questions in questions-pre 4.8. They have no posted solution, so we solve them ourselves and check every answer in python.

3
00:00:22,383 --> 00:00:36,889
The course notes call this the Under Pressure machine. The song is a duet. The pair d b stands for David Bowie, and the pair f m stands for Freddie Mercury. The regex is d b or f m, star.

4
00:00:37,339 --> 00:00:40,582
The empty string matches: zero repetitions.

5
00:00:41,032 --> 00:00:43,379
d b matches: one repetition.

6
00:00:43,829 --> 00:00:46,901
f m d b matches: two repetitions.

7
00:00:47,351 --> 00:00:49,463
Three repetitions also match.

8
00:00:49,913 --> 00:00:53,475
d alone does not match. A pair is not finished.

9
00:00:53,925 --> 00:00:58,192
b d does not match. The letters of a pair have a fixed order.

10
00:00:58,642 --> 00:01:02,695
d b f does not match. The last pair is not finished.

11
00:01:03,145 --> 00:01:11,017
d m does not match. d must be followed by b. A python check with the regex gave all eight answers.

12
00:01:11,467 --> 00:01:18,827
Here is the N F A from the course notes. It has ten states. Dashed purple edges are epsilon edges.

13
00:01:19,277 --> 00:01:21,283
State 1 is the start state.

14
00:01:21,733 --> 00:01:24,442
State 7 is the only accepting state.

15
00:01:24,892 --> 00:01:34,300
Now the templates. First the two concatenations: d then b, and f then m. Each is two constant machines joined end to end.

16
00:01:34,750 --> 00:01:43,710
Next the alternation template. State 2 is the new entry, and state 6 is the new exit. It has four epsilon edges.

17
00:01:44,160 --> 00:01:53,632
Then the repetition template. State 1 is the new entry and state 7 the new exit. 1 to 2 goes in, and 6 to 7 goes out.

18
00:01:54,082 --> 00:01:59,693
1 to 7 skips, and 6 back to 2 repeats. Every part matches a template.

19
00:02:00,143 --> 00:02:06,649
Step 2, the subset construction. The start state P is the epsilon closure of state 1.

20
00:02:07,099 --> 00:02:09,041
From 1 we reach 2 and 7.

21
00:02:09,491 --> 00:02:15,635
From 2 we reach 3 and 8. States 3, 7 and 8 have no epsilon edges.

22
00:02:16,085 --> 00:02:19,071
So P is the set 1, 2, 3, 7, 8.

23
00:02:19,521 --> 00:02:24,556
P contains 7, so P accepts. The empty string is in the language.

24
00:02:25,006 --> 00:02:29,294
Now take P, the set 1, 2, 3, 7, 8, and each letter.

25
00:02:29,744 --> 00:02:39,429
P on d. Only state 3 has a d edge, to 4. State 4 has no epsilon edges. So Q is the set with only 4.

26
00:02:39,879 --> 00:02:46,727
P on f. Only state 8 has an f edge, to 9. So R is the set with only 9.

27
00:02:47,177 --> 00:02:57,289
P on b and P on m. No state in P has those edges. Both go to the empty set, X. A pair cannot start with b or m.

28
00:02:57,739 --> 00:03:00,107
Q is the set with only state 4.

29
00:03:00,557 --> 00:03:03,117
Q on b. 4 goes to 5.

30
00:03:03,567 --> 00:03:05,274
Close: 5 reaches 6.

31
00:03:05,724 --> 00:03:07,537
6 reaches 2 and 7.

32
00:03:07,987 --> 00:03:09,801
2 reaches 3 and 8.

33
00:03:10,251 --> 00:03:15,051
The set is 2, 3, 5, 6, 7, 8. It is new, so call it S.

34
00:03:15,501 --> 00:03:20,087
S contains 7, so S accepts. One pair d b is finished.

35
00:03:20,537 --> 00:03:24,868
R is the set with only 9. On m, 9 goes to 10.

36
00:03:25,318 --> 00:03:31,398
Close the same way: 10 reaches 6, 6 reaches 2 and 7, and 2 reaches 3 and 8.

37
00:03:31,848 --> 00:03:36,755
The set is 2, 3, 6, 7, 8, 10. It is new, so call it T.

38
00:03:37,205 --> 00:03:39,679
T contains 7, so T accepts.

39
00:03:40,129 --> 00:03:43,393
S is the set 2, 3, 5, 6, 7, 8.

40
00:03:43,843 --> 00:03:49,219
S on d. Only state 3 has a d edge, to 4. That is Q again.

41
00:03:49,669 --> 00:03:55,109
S on f. Only state 8 has an f edge, to 9. That is R again.

42
00:03:55,559 --> 00:04:01,149
T also contains 3 and 8. So T on d is Q, and T on f is R.

43
00:04:01,599 --> 00:04:05,716
Q on d goes to X, because after d, only b is allowed.

44
00:04:06,166 --> 00:04:13,121
All other entries also go to X. No new sets appear. The D F A has 6 states, with X.

45
00:04:13,571 --> 00:04:17,475
Here is the full table. It has 6 states, one row each.

46
00:04:17,925 --> 00:04:21,893
P, S and T accept, because they contain state 7.

47
00:04:22,343 --> 00:04:27,249
Every row has one entry for each of the four letters. Check 2 passes.

48
00:04:27,699 --> 00:04:32,393
Here is the D F A. P goes to Q on d, and to R on f.

49
00:04:32,843 --> 00:04:36,384
Q goes to S on b. R goes to T on m.

50
00:04:36,834 --> 00:04:41,442
From S and from T, d goes back to Q, and f goes back to R.

51
00:04:41,892 --> 00:04:51,321
There are many arrows into X, so we do not draw them. Every arrow that is not drawn goes to X. The notes draw the same machine.

52
00:04:51,771 --> 00:04:58,235
Step 3, minimization. Here is the D F A as a table. It is total, because it has X.

53
00:04:58,685 --> 00:05:05,043
The notes call the bubbles G, H and X. G holds the accepting states P, S and T.

54
00:05:05,493 --> 00:05:07,413
H holds Q and R.

55
00:05:07,863 --> 00:05:09,548
X is the error state.

56
00:05:09,998 --> 00:05:13,262
Now write the target state and its bubble in each cell.

57
00:05:13,712 --> 00:05:21,648
P, S and T all go to H on d and f, and to X on b and m. So the rows in G are equal.

58
00:05:22,098 --> 00:05:30,717
Inside H, Q and R differ. On b, Q goes to G, but R goes to X. This is an ambiguous transition.

59
00:05:31,167 --> 00:05:36,201
On m, it is the other way round. Q goes to X, but R goes to G.

60
00:05:36,651 --> 00:05:41,217
So H splits into H1, which is Q, and H2, which is R.

61
00:05:41,667 --> 00:05:55,853
Round 2. P, S and T go to H1 on d, to H2 on f, and to X on b and m. Their rows are equal, so we stop. The minimal D F A has G, H1, H2 and X.

62
00:05:56,303 --> 00:06:04,239
Here is the minimal D F A, as the notes draw it after the split. G is the start, and the only accepting state.

63
00:06:04,689 --> 00:06:06,503
H1 waits for b.

64
00:06:06,953 --> 00:06:08,787
H2 waits for m.

65
00:06:09,237 --> 00:06:11,136
All other letters go to X.

66
00:06:11,586 --> 00:06:18,178
Test d b f m. G, H1, G, H2, G. It ends in G, so accept.

67
00:06:18,628 --> 00:06:25,903
Test d b f. The run stops in H2, which does not accept. The pair f m is not finished.

68
00:06:26,353 --> 00:06:30,001
Test b d. G goes to X on b. Reject.

69
00:06:30,451 --> 00:06:40,733
Six states became four. A python check ran the N F A, both D F As and the regex on every string up to length 7. They agree.

70
00:06:41,183 --> 00:06:54,410
Now the first question in questions-pre 4.8. Produce an N F A for x, y star, z or c w, a, b optional. Then convert it to a D F A and minimize it.

71
00:06:54,860 --> 00:07:03,863
This question has no posted solution. So this is our solution, and a python check confirms each step. Pause the video and try it.

72
00:07:04,313 --> 00:07:13,443
Here is our N F A, built with the templates. Concatenation joins an exit to the next entry. The letter edges are in orange.

73
00:07:13,893 --> 00:07:21,211
y star uses the repetition template: 2 in, 4 back to 3, 4 out, and 2 to 5 to skip.

74
00:07:21,661 --> 00:07:29,063
z or c w uses the alternation template: 5 is the entry and 11 is the exit.

75
00:07:29,513 --> 00:07:41,503
b optional is b or epsilon, slide 04a page 45. So it is an alternation of the b machine and the epsilon machine, 15 to 16.

76
00:07:41,953 --> 00:07:47,329
17 is the only accepting state. The N F A has 17 states.

77
00:07:47,779 --> 00:07:55,203
The start state A is the closure of state 1. State 1 has no epsilon edges, so A is just 1.

78
00:07:55,653 --> 00:07:57,957
A on x. 1 goes to 2.

79
00:07:58,407 --> 00:08:00,476
Close: 2 reaches 3 and 5.

80
00:08:00,926 --> 00:08:02,782
5 reaches 6 and 8.

81
00:08:03,232 --> 00:08:07,733
The set is 2, 3, 5, 6, 8. It is new, so call it B.

82
00:08:08,183 --> 00:08:10,530
A on every other letter goes to X.

83
00:08:10,980 --> 00:08:13,924
B is the set 2, 3, 5, 6, 8.

84
00:08:14,374 --> 00:08:16,699
B on y. 3 goes to 4.

85
00:08:17,149 --> 00:08:19,411
Close: 4 reaches 3 and 5.

86
00:08:19,861 --> 00:08:21,717
5 reaches 6 and 8.

87
00:08:22,167 --> 00:08:26,753
The set is 3, 4, 5, 6, 8. It is new, so call it C.

88
00:08:27,203 --> 00:08:34,862
B on z. 6 goes to 7, and 7 reaches 11 on an epsilon edge. So D is 7, 11.

89
00:08:35,312 --> 00:08:43,141
B on c. 8 goes to 9. State 9 has no epsilon edges. So E is the set with only 9.

90
00:08:43,591 --> 00:08:45,789
The other letters from B go to X.

91
00:08:46,239 --> 00:08:57,375
C is 3, 4, 5, 6, 8. It has the same y, z and c edges as B. So C on y is C, C on z is D, and C on c is E.

92
00:08:57,825 --> 00:09:01,707
D is 7, 11. On a, 11 goes to 12.

93
00:09:02,157 --> 00:09:04,845
Close: 12 reaches 13 and 15.

94
00:09:05,295 --> 00:09:07,023
15 reaches 16.

95
00:09:07,473 --> 00:09:09,351
16 reaches 17.

96
00:09:09,801 --> 00:09:15,497
The set is 12, 13, 15, 16, 17. It is new, so call it F.

97
00:09:15,947 --> 00:09:20,384
F contains 17, so F accepts. The b is optional.

98
00:09:20,834 --> 00:09:28,941
E is the set with only 9. On w, 9 goes to 10, and 10 reaches 11. So G is 10, 11.

99
00:09:29,391 --> 00:09:38,116
G is 10, 11. On a, 11 goes to 12. The closure is the same as before, so G on a is F again.

100
00:09:38,566 --> 00:09:41,595
F on b. 13 goes to 14.

101
00:09:42,045 --> 00:09:44,328
Close: 14 reaches 17.

102
00:09:44,778 --> 00:09:48,853
The set is 14, 17. It is new, so call it H.

103
00:09:49,303 --> 00:09:52,396
H contains 17, so H accepts.

104
00:09:52,846 --> 00:09:57,305
H has no letter edges. So every letter from H goes to X.

105
00:09:57,755 --> 00:10:01,979
No new sets appear. With X, the D F A has 9 states.

106
00:10:02,429 --> 00:10:05,799
Here is the table: nine states and seven letters.

107
00:10:06,249 --> 00:10:11,561
Most entries are X, because each state allows only one or a few letters.

108
00:10:12,011 --> 00:10:15,617
F and H accept, because they contain 17.

109
00:10:16,067 --> 00:10:18,904
Here is the D F A without the X arrows.

110
00:10:19,354 --> 00:10:24,901
B and C look alike. Both go to C on y, to D on z, and to E on c.

111
00:10:25,351 --> 00:10:31,367
D and G also look alike. Both go to F on a. Minimization will decide.

112
00:10:31,817 --> 00:10:37,918
Here is the D F A as a table, one row per state. Each cell shows the target state.

113
00:10:38,368 --> 00:10:41,461
Begin with three bubbles. F and H accept.

114
00:10:41,911 --> 00:10:43,597
X is the error state.

115
00:10:44,047 --> 00:10:47,161
All the other states are in one bubble, called rest.

116
00:10:47,611 --> 00:10:52,113
Now each cell shows the bubble of the target, not the target itself.

117
00:10:52,563 --> 00:10:59,112
F and H differ on b. F goes back into F H, but H goes to X. So they split.

118
00:10:59,562 --> 00:11:03,829
In the rest bubble, the rows fall into four groups. A reads x.

119
00:11:04,279 --> 00:11:06,561
B and C read y, z and c.

120
00:11:07,011 --> 00:11:08,782
D and G read a.

121
00:11:09,232 --> 00:11:10,704
E reads w.

122
00:11:11,154 --> 00:11:14,546
So rest splits into A, B C, D G and E.

123
00:11:14,996 --> 00:11:18,431
Round 2. Write the rows again with the new bubbles.

124
00:11:18,881 --> 00:11:24,918
B and C go to the same bubbles: B C on y, D G on z, and E on c.

125
00:11:25,368 --> 00:11:27,779
D and G both go to F on a.

126
00:11:28,229 --> 00:11:32,559
The other bubbles have one state each. Nothing splits, so we stop.

127
00:11:33,009 --> 00:11:38,172
Nine states became seven: A, B C, D G, E, F, H, and X.

128
00:11:38,622 --> 00:11:43,635
Here is the minimal D F A. It follows the regex from left to right.

129
00:11:44,085 --> 00:11:45,899
x goes from A to B C.

130
00:11:46,349 --> 00:11:48,077
B C loops on y.

131
00:11:48,527 --> 00:11:55,076
z goes from B C to D G. Or c goes to E, and then w goes to D G.

132
00:11:55,526 --> 00:11:58,641
a goes from D G to F. F accepts.

133
00:11:59,091 --> 00:12:04,147
b goes from F to H. H also accepts, so the b is optional.

134
00:12:04,597 --> 00:12:11,978
Test x y y c w a b. A, B C, B C, B C, E, D G, F, H. Accept.

135
00:12:12,428 --> 00:12:18,145
Test x z. The run ends in D G, which does not accept. The a is missing.

136
00:12:18,595 --> 00:12:27,043
A python check ran all the machines and the regex on every string over the seven letters, up to length 6. They agree.

137
00:12:27,493 --> 00:12:36,624
The second question. Produce an N F A for r m b or c, optional, then n s f star. Then convert and minimize.

138
00:12:37,074 --> 00:12:43,026
Again there is no posted solution. This is our solution. Pause the video and try it.

139
00:12:43,476 --> 00:12:48,831
Here is our N F A. The letter edges are r m b, c, and n s f.

140
00:12:49,281 --> 00:12:54,678
The inner alternation, r m b or c, has entry 2 and exit 11.

141
00:12:55,128 --> 00:13:07,480
The question mark means: this or epsilon. So a second alternation, with entry 1 and exit 12, picks either the inner machine or the epsilon machine from 9 to 10.

142
00:13:07,930 --> 00:13:19,599
Then n s f star uses the repetition template: 12 in to 13, 16 back to 13, 16 out to 17, and 12 to 17 to skip.

143
00:13:20,049 --> 00:13:22,673
17 is the only accepting state.

144
00:13:23,123 --> 00:13:26,878
The start state A is the epsilon closure of state 1.

145
00:13:27,328 --> 00:13:29,184
From 1 we reach 2 and 9.

146
00:13:29,634 --> 00:13:31,810
From 2 we reach 3 and 7.

147
00:13:32,260 --> 00:13:34,009
From 9 we reach 10.

148
00:13:34,459 --> 00:13:36,294
From 10 we reach 12.

149
00:13:36,744 --> 00:13:39,603
From 12 we reach 13 and 17.

150
00:13:40,053 --> 00:13:44,895
So A is 1, 2, 3, 7, 9, 10, 12, 13, 17.

151
00:13:45,345 --> 00:13:49,164
A contains 17, so A accepts the empty string.

152
00:13:49,614 --> 00:13:51,491
Now take A and each letter.

153
00:13:51,941 --> 00:13:59,792
A on r. 3 goes to 4. State 4 has no epsilon edges. So B is the set with only 4.

154
00:14:00,242 --> 00:14:02,674
A on c. 7 goes to 8.

155
00:14:03,124 --> 00:14:04,959
Close: 8 reaches 11.

156
00:14:05,409 --> 00:14:07,073
11 reaches 12.

157
00:14:07,523 --> 00:14:09,805
12 reaches 13 and 17.

158
00:14:10,255 --> 00:14:15,823
The set is 8, 11, 12, 13, 17. It is new, so call it C.

159
00:14:16,273 --> 00:14:19,025
C contains 17, so C accepts.

160
00:14:19,475 --> 00:14:27,091
A on n. 13 goes to 14. So D is the set with only 14. The optional part was skipped.

161
00:14:27,541 --> 00:14:30,571
A on m, b, s or f goes to X.

162
00:14:31,021 --> 00:14:41,047
B is the set with only 4. On m, 4 goes to 5. State 5 has no epsilon edges, so E is the set with only 5.

163
00:14:41,497 --> 00:14:44,015
E on b. 5 goes to 6.

164
00:14:44,465 --> 00:14:46,406
Close: 6 reaches 11.

165
00:14:46,856 --> 00:14:48,520
11 reaches 12.

166
00:14:48,970 --> 00:14:51,253
12 reaches 13 and 17.

167
00:14:51,703 --> 00:14:57,143
The set is 6, 11, 12, 13, 17. It is new, so call it F.

168
00:14:57,593 --> 00:15:00,643
F contains 17, so F accepts.

169
00:15:01,093 --> 00:15:09,072
D is the set with only 14. On s, 14 goes to 15. So G is the set with only 15.

170
00:15:09,522 --> 00:15:12,530
G on f. 15 goes to 16.

171
00:15:12,980 --> 00:15:16,095
Close: 16 reaches 13 and 17.

172
00:15:16,545 --> 00:15:21,259
The set is 13, 16, 17. It is new, so call it H.

173
00:15:21,709 --> 00:15:24,803
H contains 17, so H accepts.

174
00:15:25,253 --> 00:15:30,202
C, F and H all contain 13. So each one goes to D on n.

175
00:15:30,652 --> 00:15:37,137
All other entries go to X. No new sets appear. With X, the D F A has 9 states.

176
00:15:37,587 --> 00:15:40,937
Here is the table. Nine states and seven letters.

177
00:15:41,387 --> 00:15:45,568
A, C, F and H accept, because they contain 17.

178
00:15:46,018 --> 00:15:48,855
Here is the D F A without the X arrows.

179
00:15:49,305 --> 00:15:53,593
C, F and H look alike. Each one only goes to D on n.

180
00:15:54,043 --> 00:15:58,587
Here is the D F A as a table. Each cell shows the target state.

181
00:15:59,037 --> 00:16:02,600
Three bubbles again. A, C, F and H accept.

182
00:16:03,050 --> 00:16:05,247
B, D, E and G do not.

183
00:16:05,697 --> 00:16:07,383
X is the error state.

184
00:16:07,833 --> 00:16:10,606
Now each cell shows the bubble of the target.

185
00:16:11,056 --> 00:16:15,429
A differs from C, F and H. A also reads r and c.

186
00:16:15,879 --> 00:16:23,794
In the other bubble, each state reads a different letter. B reads m, D reads s, E reads b, and G reads f.

187
00:16:24,244 --> 00:16:29,855
So A splits away from C F H, and B D E G splits into four bubbles.

188
00:16:30,305 --> 00:16:33,739
Round 2. Write the rows again with the new bubbles.

189
00:16:34,189 --> 00:16:40,291
C, F and H have equal rows: n goes to D, and every other letter goes to X.

190
00:16:40,741 --> 00:16:45,071
The other bubbles have one state each. Nothing splits, so we stop.

191
00:16:45,521 --> 00:16:50,620
Nine states became seven: A, B, E, C F H, D, G, and X.

192
00:16:51,070 --> 00:16:57,107
Here is the minimal D F A. From A, the path r m b leads to C F H.

193
00:16:57,557 --> 00:17:00,672
The letter c also leads from A to C F H.

194
00:17:01,122 --> 00:17:06,882
The letter n leads from A straight into the loop n s f: D, G, then C F H.

195
00:17:07,332 --> 00:17:13,988
Test r m b n s f. A, B, E, C F H, D, G, C F H. Accept.

196
00:17:14,438 --> 00:17:23,270
Test n s f c. The loop ends in C F H, and c goes to X. The optional part must come first. Reject.

197
00:17:23,720 --> 00:17:30,440
A python check ran all the machines and the regex on every string up to length 6. They agree.

198
00:17:30,890 --> 00:17:40,853
To summarize. An optional part, x question mark, is x or epsilon. Build it with the alternation template and the epsilon machine.

199
00:17:41,303 --> 00:17:49,729
With a large alphabet, most arrows go to the error state. Draw X once, and say that every missing arrow goes to X.

200
00:17:50,179 --> 00:17:59,161
Minimize with X in the machine. States with equal rows stay together. Here: B and C, D and G, and C, F, H.

201
00:17:59,611 --> 00:18:07,504
The state counts were 10, 6, 4 for Under Pressure, and 17, 9, 7 for both questions in 4.8.

202
00:18:07,954 --> 00:18:20,583
Always test some accepted and some rejected strings on every machine and on the regex. The next video is a hard design question: binary numbers divisible by 5.
